WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.475
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.930 --> 00:00:08.530
ELSIE KENYON: Hello, everyone.

00:00:08.530 --> 00:00:12.750
My name is Elsie Kenyon, and
I'm excited to be here today

00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:16.530
with my colleagues
from Two Sigma.

00:00:16.530 --> 00:00:20.220
Now, Two Sigma is an
investment manager

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:23.070
which applies data
science and technology

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:26.710
to find value in the
world's financial markets.

00:00:26.710 --> 00:00:31.830
So why, you may ask, are we
here today to talk about games?

00:00:31.830 --> 00:00:36.730
Well, in Two Sigma's
17-year cultural history,

00:00:36.730 --> 00:00:40.530
there have been many
successful internal challenges

00:00:40.530 --> 00:00:43.170
organized around games.

00:00:43.170 --> 00:00:48.960
You may have seen a YouTube
video of our air hockey robot

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:50.550
competition.

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:54.090
We found that these games-- and
particularly games that pair

00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:56.220
humans with machines--

00:00:56.220 --> 00:01:01.590
are really unique opportunities
for applying new skills

00:01:01.590 --> 00:01:05.360
to difficult challenges.

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:09.290
Two years ago, Two Sigma
interns, Ben and Michael, here

00:01:09.290 --> 00:01:13.850
today, created an entirely
new such game, Halite,

00:01:13.850 --> 00:01:20.630
in which players build bots
that play the games for them.

00:01:20.630 --> 00:01:23.900
The Halite Artificial
Intelligence Competition

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:26.630
was so successful
within Two Sigma

00:01:26.630 --> 00:01:30.500
that we opened up the
competition globally,

00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:34.430
open sourced the code base,
and repeated the process

00:01:34.430 --> 00:01:37.220
the next year with
a brand new game.

00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:40.430
This fall will launch the
Third Annual Halite Artificial

00:01:40.430 --> 00:01:42.370
Intelligence Competition.

00:01:42.370 --> 00:01:45.560
Now, to date, there have
been thousands of players

00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:49.400
from over 100 countries
that have played Halite.

00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:51.560
These player-submitted
bots have been

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:55.220
written in over 20
different languages

00:01:55.220 --> 00:01:58.960
and have played
millions of games.

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:02.740
Now, on the one hand, a
global programming competition

00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:06.610
has provided Two Sigma with
a new source for identifying

00:02:06.610 --> 00:02:11.590
strong talent, but there
is another stronger promise

00:02:11.590 --> 00:02:13.150
of Halite.

00:02:13.150 --> 00:02:15.520
At the top of this
Halite II leaderboard,

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:19.150
you'll see professionals,
university students,

00:02:19.150 --> 00:02:22.850
and even a high school
student at number four.

00:02:22.850 --> 00:02:26.710
We've seen players of all ages
and experience levels engaged

00:02:26.710 --> 00:02:29.020
with advanced algorithms,
AI, and machine

00:02:29.020 --> 00:02:32.650
learning via this
immersive game on their own

00:02:32.650 --> 00:02:33.860
and with each other.

00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:40.980
This is Halite I.
The rules of the game

00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:46.110
can be explained in a
paragraph, but the game

00:02:46.110 --> 00:02:50.940
has a branching factor many
times that of the game of Go.

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:53.910
It's an open-ended environment
in which many strategies

00:02:53.910 --> 00:02:55.830
can be successful.

00:02:55.830 --> 00:02:58.650
This laboratory-like
nature is a feature

00:02:58.650 --> 00:03:02.820
of Halite, which you'll
understand further

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:06.900
as Ben and Michael contextualize
Halite in the history of games

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:11.370
and share the principles
guiding Halite's development.

00:03:11.370 --> 00:03:15.060
Running Halite requires
significant back end resources.

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:19.200
Jack will explain how Google
Cloud enabled Two Sigma

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:25.830
to run a global AI competition
at scale with no downtime.

00:03:25.830 --> 00:03:29.520
We consider Halite a game
designed for this era in AI

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:33.040
and this era in technology,
and we hope by the end of today

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:34.530
that you will, too.

00:03:34.530 --> 00:03:37.350
Now I'll hand it over
to Michael and Ben.

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:38.290
MICHAEL: Thank you.

00:03:38.290 --> 00:03:41.370
BEN: Thanks, Elsie.

00:03:41.370 --> 00:03:43.140
So-- back to one.

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:44.280
OK.

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:47.160
So we're going to talk a little
bit about the Halite games,

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:49.720
the predecessors, the process,
the principles, and all

00:03:49.720 --> 00:03:50.250
of that.

00:03:50.250 --> 00:03:51.990
We want to start
with an introduction

00:03:51.990 --> 00:03:53.700
of the evolution
of games and how

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:56.790
Halite fits into that history.

00:03:56.790 --> 00:03:59.250
And as you know, all
presentations need a cute

00:03:59.250 --> 00:04:01.770
picture of cats to
succeed-- here is ours--

00:04:01.770 --> 00:04:05.490
but it also has an actual
meaning for the presentation,

00:04:05.490 --> 00:04:07.820
too.

00:04:07.820 --> 00:04:13.440
I think we often conceptualize
of games as a uniquely

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:14.850
human thing, but they're not.

00:04:14.850 --> 00:04:16.829
We see games with
animals all the time.

00:04:16.829 --> 00:04:19.279
Here, these cubs
are play wrestling

00:04:19.279 --> 00:04:22.590
so that when they have to
wrestle for real later in life,

00:04:22.590 --> 00:04:24.870
they'll be ready.

00:04:24.870 --> 00:04:29.070
We think that this leads to
an important use of games--

00:04:29.070 --> 00:04:32.520
that really, games, under one
viewpoint, under one lens,

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:36.750
are about learning, that a game
is an environment with very

00:04:36.750 --> 00:04:40.930
low risk in which you can
try new strategies and ideas,

00:04:40.930 --> 00:04:45.400
explore, hone your skills, so
that when it actually matters,

00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:48.000
you'll be ready.

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:52.560
And continuing on in that
thread, just a little bit

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:55.100
about some early strategy
games that stick around today,

00:04:55.100 --> 00:04:58.969
there are earlier games
like Egyptian games of dice

00:04:58.969 --> 00:05:01.260
that precede these games,
but the ones that have really

00:05:01.260 --> 00:05:04.290
remained popular are these games
of strategies-- primarily Go

00:05:04.290 --> 00:05:07.590
and chess, which originated
about 2,500 years ago,

00:05:07.590 --> 00:05:11.160
and then chess about
1,000 years later.

00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:15.882
And what's key about these
games is that, in their time,

00:05:15.882 --> 00:05:18.090
they've both been considered
these tools for learning

00:05:18.090 --> 00:05:20.100
strategy that, as a
military commander,

00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:22.860
you'd be expected to be
good at chess in addition

00:05:22.860 --> 00:05:24.120
to a good military commander.

00:05:24.120 --> 00:05:26.010
There was thought that
there is some transfer

00:05:26.010 --> 00:05:27.330
of the skill between them.

00:05:27.330 --> 00:05:30.090
But that also, we see games
kind of come into their own

00:05:30.090 --> 00:05:33.480
as a form of art.

00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:36.360
I think we might have this
idea that games as art

00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:40.020
is very modern, in that now we
have all sorts of video games

00:05:40.020 --> 00:05:44.010
that are artistic and take
you through some story and all

00:05:44.010 --> 00:05:45.630
that, but really,
this originates

00:05:45.630 --> 00:05:49.470
a lot earlier with these games.

00:05:49.470 --> 00:05:52.920
MICHAEL: All right, so the
history of games and machines

00:05:52.920 --> 00:05:55.740
is definitely a lot shorter than
that of the history of humans

00:05:55.740 --> 00:05:57.210
playing games.

00:05:57.210 --> 00:06:00.820
It's about 50 years versus
many thousands of years.

00:06:00.820 --> 00:06:02.520
But it is a dense
history, nonetheless,

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:05.070
and it mostly consists
of machines beating

00:06:05.070 --> 00:06:06.510
humans at their own games.

00:06:06.510 --> 00:06:09.600
The famous example is Deep
Blue becoming superhuman

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:13.220
on chess, and recently
AlphaGo beating Lee Sedol,

00:06:13.220 --> 00:06:15.300
and soon, probably,
Dota 2 in StarCraft

00:06:15.300 --> 00:06:18.660
will be beat by open AI
and DeepMind, respectively.

00:06:18.660 --> 00:06:20.880
But what we're really
interested in with Halite

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:24.570
in building this competition
is making games that leverage

00:06:24.570 --> 00:06:28.320
both the best parts of humans
and the best parts of machines.

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:30.090
We want to design
games where humans

00:06:30.090 --> 00:06:31.665
need to think through
the strategies,

00:06:31.665 --> 00:06:34.530
think through the rules,
think through the game logic,

00:06:34.530 --> 00:06:39.905
and then design systems that are
executed by their bots nicely.

00:06:39.905 --> 00:06:41.280
BEN: Yeah.

00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:44.250
I think that there's
often a conception

00:06:44.250 --> 00:06:48.912
of the progress of AI as sort
of antagonistic to humans,

00:06:48.912 --> 00:06:51.120
that it's like, well, all
right, that's another thing

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:52.950
that machines are
better at than humans.

00:06:52.950 --> 00:06:55.230
When's Skynet coming?

00:06:55.230 --> 00:06:58.430
But we don't see it that way.

00:06:58.430 --> 00:07:03.240
There's no reason that it has
to be adversarial like that.

00:07:03.240 --> 00:07:06.870
One could easily imagine
a future in which humans

00:07:06.870 --> 00:07:09.390
and machines coexist
on the same games

00:07:09.390 --> 00:07:11.690
and learn from them
together in different ways,

00:07:11.690 --> 00:07:13.275
and we're excited
by that prospect.

00:07:16.200 --> 00:07:18.806
So, we're going to talk
first very briefly about some

00:07:18.806 --> 00:07:21.180
of the predecessors of Halite,
get into the Halite games,

00:07:21.180 --> 00:07:25.380
and then talk about the
principles behind them.

00:07:25.380 --> 00:07:29.280
So, diving in, we start with
the Multi-Agent Programming

00:07:29.280 --> 00:07:31.680
Contest, which is a
yearly competition that's

00:07:31.680 --> 00:07:33.780
run since about 2005.

00:07:33.780 --> 00:07:36.680
And the key contribution
that this really adds,

00:07:36.680 --> 00:07:41.130
we think, to the table,
is the multi-agent aspect.

00:07:41.130 --> 00:07:43.821
When you're controlling a bunch
of different agents at once,

00:07:43.821 --> 00:07:45.570
the kind of techniques
that one has to use

00:07:45.570 --> 00:07:48.450
are very different than those
when it's a single agent.

00:07:48.450 --> 00:07:51.390
With a single agent, very
restricted number of actions,

00:07:51.390 --> 00:07:54.330
it's usually pretty
easy to do pretty well

00:07:54.330 --> 00:07:57.500
with something like Monte
Carlo or Minimax methods

00:07:57.500 --> 00:08:02.190
and just some sort of valuation
function learned or hard-coded.

00:08:02.190 --> 00:08:09.540
But once you explode the
state space with more agents,

00:08:09.540 --> 00:08:12.580
the techniques that one has
to use become very different

00:08:12.580 --> 00:08:15.300
and the multi-agent programming
contests really open that up.

00:08:15.300 --> 00:08:16.883
MICHAEL: And
additionally, these games

00:08:16.883 --> 00:08:19.200
are very hard for humans to
play in a vanilla fashion.

00:08:19.200 --> 00:08:22.560
Unless they write either
heuristics to help guide them

00:08:22.560 --> 00:08:25.860
through the game, or they
write boxes that are executors,

00:08:25.860 --> 00:08:29.970
they are essentially unable to
evaluate who's winning the game

00:08:29.970 --> 00:08:31.320
and decide what they should do.

00:08:31.320 --> 00:08:31.820
BEN: Yeah.

00:08:31.820 --> 00:08:34.559
And actually, one last point
about the multi-agent nature--

00:08:34.559 --> 00:08:37.320
the fact that it is
hard for humans to play

00:08:37.320 --> 00:08:39.919
is also a great motivator
on the educational aspect

00:08:39.919 --> 00:08:40.760
of these games.

00:08:40.760 --> 00:08:43.135
If you're trying to encourage
someone young to get really

00:08:43.135 --> 00:08:44.718
interested in
programming, and they're

00:08:44.718 --> 00:08:46.940
like, what's the point of
this, like, why do I care,

00:08:46.940 --> 00:08:48.650
having them see
this kind of a game

00:08:48.650 --> 00:08:51.620
that they can't actually
play effectively

00:08:51.620 --> 00:08:53.980
but looks interesting and
that they might want to play

00:08:53.980 --> 00:08:55.250
and then showing
them that there's

00:08:55.250 --> 00:08:57.875
a path to this through computers
is a really effective teaching

00:08:57.875 --> 00:09:00.440
tool, so that's another
thing that comes out of this.

00:09:00.440 --> 00:09:01.130
MICHAEL: Yes.

00:09:01.130 --> 00:09:05.120
The second predecessor to
Halite is the Arimaa Challenge,

00:09:05.120 --> 00:09:07.970
which is the derivative of
chess that's made explicitly

00:09:07.970 --> 00:09:12.770
hard for game-playing AI.

00:09:12.770 --> 00:09:15.650
It's really impressive
since humans were actually

00:09:15.650 --> 00:09:17.900
the best players of
this game up until 2015,

00:09:17.900 --> 00:09:19.537
even though the game
is pretty simple.

00:09:19.537 --> 00:09:20.870
The state space is pretty small.

00:09:20.870 --> 00:09:23.024
You're only making
one move per turn.

00:09:23.024 --> 00:09:24.440
But still, evaluating
the board is

00:09:24.440 --> 00:09:26.595
extremely hard for computers.

00:09:26.595 --> 00:09:28.220
And so we like to
think that we drew on

00:09:28.220 --> 00:09:31.400
both detractibilty and also
the difficulty of Arimaa

00:09:31.400 --> 00:09:32.610
in making Halite.

00:09:32.610 --> 00:09:33.530
BEN: Yeah.

00:09:33.530 --> 00:09:37.070
Probably the most direct
spiritual predecessor of Halite

00:09:37.070 --> 00:09:40.005
is the UWaterloo and
Google AI Challenge.

00:09:40.005 --> 00:09:41.630
This is the ants
competition, which was

00:09:41.630 --> 00:09:43.340
the third and final year of it.

00:09:43.340 --> 00:09:48.349
And what this did was it took
these previous multi-agent

00:09:48.349 --> 00:09:50.390
competitions, and it just
completely opened it up

00:09:50.390 --> 00:09:52.920
to the massive online scale.

00:09:52.920 --> 00:09:57.770
Now we have thousands of users,
whereas normally, these older

00:09:57.770 --> 00:10:00.440
programming contests were like,
you're on some mailing list,

00:10:00.440 --> 00:10:02.420
they send it out, you
think it's kind of cool,

00:10:02.420 --> 00:10:03.920
you develop it for
a month, and then

00:10:03.920 --> 00:10:06.045
you submit to some conference,
and they ranked them

00:10:06.045 --> 00:10:11.460
all in some side list
on something or another.

00:10:11.460 --> 00:10:14.390
Now we can engage with
thousands of players.

00:10:14.390 --> 00:10:18.140
And an important contribution is
also the real-time leader board

00:10:18.140 --> 00:10:20.390
from the AI
challenge, and this is

00:10:20.390 --> 00:10:23.870
what enables it to be
an educational tool that

00:10:23.870 --> 00:10:27.518
extends broadly beyond just a
couple of individual academics.

00:10:31.820 --> 00:10:34.790
So that brings us to,
finally, Vindinium,

00:10:34.790 --> 00:10:37.790
which is the last
of our predecessors

00:10:37.790 --> 00:10:38.630
that we'll mention.

00:10:38.630 --> 00:10:40.690
It also ran concurrently
with Halite I,

00:10:40.690 --> 00:10:43.430
so there's elements
of that, too.

00:10:43.430 --> 00:10:46.040
And one of the really nice
things about Vindinium--

00:10:46.040 --> 00:10:49.995
even though it's a single-agent
game and differs from Halite

00:10:49.995 --> 00:10:50.960
in a bunch of ways--

00:10:50.960 --> 00:10:54.680
there's a lot of core
infrastructural differences--

00:10:54.680 --> 00:10:56.390
it's really nice visuals.

00:10:56.390 --> 00:10:58.490
You see it, and
you're intrigued,

00:10:58.490 --> 00:11:01.370
and you want to know
what's going on--

00:11:01.370 --> 00:11:03.874
the sprites moving around--
it's very appealing.

00:11:03.874 --> 00:11:06.290
MICHAEL: Yeah, and we like to
think that in making Halite,

00:11:06.290 --> 00:11:09.156
we took something from
all four of these games.

00:11:09.156 --> 00:11:11.030
From the Multi-Agent
Programming Competition,

00:11:11.030 --> 00:11:14.330
we took the genre of
multi-agent games, from Arimaa,

00:11:14.330 --> 00:11:17.990
we took the difficulty of the
game, from the AI Challenge,

00:11:17.990 --> 00:11:20.270
we took the continuous
global leaderboard,

00:11:20.270 --> 00:11:23.780
and from Vindinium we took
the visual appeal and theming.

00:11:23.780 --> 00:11:27.170
And this leads in nicely to
our description of Halite

00:11:27.170 --> 00:11:28.380
I and Halite II.

00:11:28.380 --> 00:11:29.085
BEN: Yeah.

00:11:29.085 --> 00:11:30.710
So we'll start off
with Halite I, which

00:11:30.710 --> 00:11:34.310
ran between 2016 and 2017.

00:11:34.310 --> 00:11:37.550
MICHAEL: It had
about 1,600 users

00:11:37.550 --> 00:11:39.810
over the course of three months.

00:11:39.810 --> 00:11:42.190
BEN: And we'll talk
about [INAUDIBLE],,

00:11:42.190 --> 00:11:43.834
and I'll just get them out.

00:11:43.834 --> 00:11:45.750
So, we're going to start
with the rules of it,

00:11:45.750 --> 00:11:47.750
just to give a general
overview of them.

00:11:47.750 --> 00:11:49.730
What you have here are
three different players

00:11:49.730 --> 00:11:53.060
on this map, this 2D
grid, and each one

00:11:53.060 --> 00:11:55.490
has the objective of
taking over the entire map.

00:11:55.490 --> 00:11:59.820
Players are moving each
individual piece up,

00:11:59.820 --> 00:12:03.200
down, left, or right each turn,
or telling it to remain still.

00:12:03.200 --> 00:12:05.480
When they remain
still, the pieces grow.

00:12:05.480 --> 00:12:07.820
You can see that as the size
of the square increasing.

00:12:07.820 --> 00:12:09.890
And when they interact
with opponents,

00:12:09.890 --> 00:12:13.040
they fight and shrink.

00:12:13.040 --> 00:12:15.620
So, for starters,
you'll immediately

00:12:15.620 --> 00:12:16.860
notice this abstract design.

00:12:16.860 --> 00:12:22.130
It's very mathematical,
geometric in theming.

00:12:22.130 --> 00:12:26.240
And, in fact, the only real
connection it has to any theme

00:12:26.240 --> 00:12:28.287
whatsoever is the name Halite.

00:12:28.287 --> 00:12:29.870
The name Halite came
from-- it sort of

00:12:29.870 --> 00:12:32.000
looked like these
cubic crystals growing

00:12:32.000 --> 00:12:34.850
was one of the analogies
that we thought of.

00:12:34.850 --> 00:12:38.600
But a couple of important
aspects of this--

00:12:38.600 --> 00:12:41.360
we have the really
simple rules and commands

00:12:41.360 --> 00:12:44.090
that you just heard
basically all of the rules.

00:12:44.090 --> 00:12:45.350
There's not that much of it.

00:12:45.350 --> 00:12:47.930
You can easily explain it
in well under a paragraph.

00:12:47.930 --> 00:12:49.460
And also the commands--

00:12:49.460 --> 00:12:52.420
because you don't have multiple
types of units or anything,

00:12:52.420 --> 00:12:54.310
you're just telling
your pieces where to go.

00:12:54.310 --> 00:12:55.850
It's really easy to get started.

00:12:55.850 --> 00:12:58.280
A side effect of this is
you have a very clean API

00:12:58.280 --> 00:12:59.330
for programming.

00:12:59.330 --> 00:13:02.060
You can have a Python bot
up in literally 10 lines

00:13:02.060 --> 00:13:05.660
of Python, which is
just-- it's nice.

00:13:05.660 --> 00:13:07.329
We also have the
discrete grid space.

00:13:07.329 --> 00:13:08.870
This makes it very
easy for beginners

00:13:08.870 --> 00:13:12.020
because arrays are such a
natural programming tool

00:13:12.020 --> 00:13:14.760
to work with.

00:13:14.760 --> 00:13:17.090
And finally, we have the
emergent behaviors of it.

00:13:17.090 --> 00:13:18.680
But even though the
rules are simple,

00:13:18.680 --> 00:13:20.180
there is a lot to
do in this game.

00:13:20.180 --> 00:13:22.970
From working with the
combat optimization

00:13:22.970 --> 00:13:25.700
to deciding when you want to
grow your pieces or move them,

00:13:25.700 --> 00:13:27.920
or doing traffic control
of all of your pieces,

00:13:27.920 --> 00:13:29.630
or deciding when
you want to expand

00:13:29.630 --> 00:13:31.760
your territory versus
attack your opponents

00:13:31.760 --> 00:13:33.500
and conquer new
territory, there's

00:13:33.500 --> 00:13:35.060
a whole bunch of
separate problems

00:13:35.060 --> 00:13:37.880
in this that all come from
these very simple rules,

00:13:37.880 --> 00:13:40.400
and that's what makes this
game so unique and interesting.

00:13:40.400 --> 00:13:42.170
MICHAEL: Yeah, and when
we're designing these games,

00:13:42.170 --> 00:13:44.660
we like to say that we want
the players to play each other

00:13:44.660 --> 00:13:46.552
and not to play the game.

00:13:46.552 --> 00:13:48.260
And so when you make
the rule set simple,

00:13:48.260 --> 00:13:50.990
when you're not just making
a bloated game with a bunch

00:13:50.990 --> 00:13:54.050
of roadblocks, a bunch
of constructed problems

00:13:54.050 --> 00:13:57.380
for players to get
through, you leave open

00:13:57.380 --> 00:13:59.630
the possibility of
responding to other players

00:13:59.630 --> 00:14:01.622
and having these nice
emergent behaviors.

00:14:01.622 --> 00:14:03.080
And so this brings
us to Halite II.

00:14:03.080 --> 00:14:04.610
This ran last year.

00:14:04.610 --> 00:14:07.940
It had over 6,000 users.

00:14:07.940 --> 00:14:10.850
And the game broadly
works like this.

00:14:10.850 --> 00:14:14.150
You were the admiral
of a fleet of ships.

00:14:14.150 --> 00:14:15.920
Every turn, you look
at all of your ships,

00:14:15.920 --> 00:14:19.200
and you set the velocity
of each of them.

00:14:19.200 --> 00:14:22.310
In addition-- and so the ships
exist within a continuous space

00:14:22.310 --> 00:14:24.156
of movement.

00:14:24.156 --> 00:14:26.280
In addition to moving, the
ships can do two things.

00:14:26.280 --> 00:14:28.250
They can dock on
planets and planets

00:14:28.250 --> 00:14:30.710
produce more ships
proportional to the number

00:14:30.710 --> 00:14:33.140
of ships docked on the
planet, and they can also

00:14:33.140 --> 00:14:34.070
fight other ships.

00:14:34.070 --> 00:14:37.010
So loosely, when two ships
are within a certain radius,

00:14:37.010 --> 00:14:39.420
they will automatically
attack each other.

00:14:39.420 --> 00:14:41.150
And so the game
was, in many ways,

00:14:41.150 --> 00:14:42.980
a response to
Halite I. We wanted

00:14:42.980 --> 00:14:46.760
to try out some different
sorts of game design mechanics,

00:14:46.760 --> 00:14:48.800
and one of these
is that it exists

00:14:48.800 --> 00:14:50.840
within a continuous
space of movement.

00:14:50.840 --> 00:14:55.250
And the advantage of this is
that users have more solutions

00:14:55.250 --> 00:14:57.100
that they can try out.

00:14:57.100 --> 00:14:59.270
Combat code and
navigation are actually

00:14:59.270 --> 00:15:02.840
going to be more
complex because of this.

00:15:02.840 --> 00:15:05.000
The drawback is that,
programmatically,

00:15:05.000 --> 00:15:06.920
in terms of the API,
it's more difficult.

00:15:06.920 --> 00:15:08.080
It's worse for beginners.

00:15:08.080 --> 00:15:09.580
And so, because of
this, we actually

00:15:09.580 --> 00:15:12.620
are probably reverting to
a grid in Halite III, which

00:15:12.620 --> 00:15:14.670
will come out in the
fall, we should mention,

00:15:14.670 --> 00:15:19.520
and you should go
sign up at halite.io.

00:15:19.520 --> 00:15:22.950
Yes, and also combat was much
more difficult in Halite II.

00:15:22.950 --> 00:15:24.470
In Halite I, it was very local.

00:15:24.470 --> 00:15:28.280
It was, if you move a piece
onto another, they fight.

00:15:28.280 --> 00:15:30.970
But in Halite II, you had
to do global optimization

00:15:30.970 --> 00:15:34.110
to do combat properly.

00:15:34.110 --> 00:15:35.710
Players actually
called it flocking.

00:15:35.710 --> 00:15:38.234
It's when you have a group
of ships stay together,

00:15:38.234 --> 00:15:39.900
and they single out
lone opponent ships.

00:15:39.900 --> 00:15:42.960
They surround them and
they destroy the ship.

00:15:42.960 --> 00:15:44.790
And then one pretty
obvious change

00:15:44.790 --> 00:15:47.310
between Halite I and Halite II
is that now we have a theme.

00:15:47.310 --> 00:15:49.410
We're actually tying
this to space wars,

00:15:49.410 --> 00:15:51.690
and there are a couple
of advantages of that.

00:15:51.690 --> 00:15:53.910
One, it's probably
easier for you all,

00:15:53.910 --> 00:15:55.470
just looking at
the two games, not

00:15:55.470 --> 00:15:57.390
knowing what they are,
to understand what's

00:15:57.390 --> 00:15:58.890
happening in Halite
II versus what's

00:15:58.890 --> 00:16:01.610
happening in Halite I. You
see ships, you see planets,

00:16:01.610 --> 00:16:03.940
you see space.

00:16:03.940 --> 00:16:05.940
And because of that,
we can actually

00:16:05.940 --> 00:16:07.920
get away with some
more complex rules,

00:16:07.920 --> 00:16:10.440
because when you think of
actually how physical movement

00:16:10.440 --> 00:16:13.050
works, when you're
controlling a ship's lofty,

00:16:13.050 --> 00:16:14.730
it's mathematically
pretty complicated.

00:16:14.730 --> 00:16:16.740
But humans understand
how it works,

00:16:16.740 --> 00:16:18.690
they come into it
with that intuition,

00:16:18.690 --> 00:16:21.344
and we can get away with
a more interesting game.

00:16:21.344 --> 00:16:23.760
And then finally, an important
thing we did with Halite II

00:16:23.760 --> 00:16:26.460
is we took away AI
programmers, or deep

00:16:26.460 --> 00:16:27.870
learning programmers,
only excuse

00:16:27.870 --> 00:16:30.030
for not writing good bots.

00:16:30.030 --> 00:16:32.250
That is, we provided them GPUs.

00:16:32.250 --> 00:16:35.891
So on our servers, they
have basically the compute

00:16:35.891 --> 00:16:37.890
they need to run whatever
gigantic convolutional

00:16:37.890 --> 00:16:41.610
[INAUDIBLE] networks they
want or deep RL systems,

00:16:41.610 --> 00:16:46.040
and we saw players use
that in very cool ways.

00:16:46.040 --> 00:16:50.160
And, yeah, so this brings
us to a section on--

00:16:50.160 --> 00:16:53.075
we're going to describe loosely
how we designed these games.

00:16:53.075 --> 00:16:54.075
Go over some principles.

00:16:54.075 --> 00:16:57.319
BEN: Yeah, so I'll start
with the principles.

00:16:57.319 --> 00:16:58.860
So the first principle
of these games

00:16:58.860 --> 00:17:00.120
is they have to be simple.

00:17:00.120 --> 00:17:01.953
There's 10,000 things
to do on the internet.

00:17:01.953 --> 00:17:03.180
Why should you do Halite?

00:17:03.180 --> 00:17:05.944
Even if you should do Halite
and the rules were complicated,

00:17:05.944 --> 00:17:08.310
it's really hard for us to
convince you to do Halite

00:17:08.310 --> 00:17:10.260
if you have to spend
two hours learning

00:17:10.260 --> 00:17:13.170
the ins and outs of some really
complicated set of rules.

00:17:13.170 --> 00:17:17.349
So we took inspiration from the
game of Go with respect to this

00:17:17.349 --> 00:17:22.020
that it's such an incredibly
simple set of rules

00:17:22.020 --> 00:17:24.329
to work with, yet
it's been incredibly

00:17:24.329 --> 00:17:28.349
challenging and interesting
for over thousands of years.

00:17:28.349 --> 00:17:31.266
We really wanted to emulate
something like that.

00:17:31.266 --> 00:17:32.640
Another side effect
of simplicity

00:17:32.640 --> 00:17:35.380
is that you get a game
which is fast to compute,

00:17:35.380 --> 00:17:37.592
which is usually--

00:17:37.592 --> 00:17:39.550
well, it's advantageous
for a bunch of reasons.

00:17:39.550 --> 00:17:40.830
First, we are running
tens of millions

00:17:40.830 --> 00:17:42.090
of games on our server.

00:17:42.090 --> 00:17:44.520
Fast compute lowers our
cost, which is nice.

00:17:44.520 --> 00:17:46.320
It also makes it easier
for bot developers.

00:17:46.320 --> 00:17:50.490
When they're testing locally
and working on their bot,

00:17:50.490 --> 00:17:54.120
if they can run 100 games really
quickly between their own bots,

00:17:54.120 --> 00:17:55.980
it makes it much easier
for them to iterate,

00:17:55.980 --> 00:17:58.170
and we get better, more
interesting submissions

00:17:58.170 --> 00:18:01.361
by the end of the competition.

00:18:01.361 --> 00:18:04.590
A second principle is that these
games need to be very visual.

00:18:04.590 --> 00:18:08.670
We like these bright colors
on darkish backgrounds

00:18:08.670 --> 00:18:09.630
that pop out.

00:18:09.630 --> 00:18:11.490
You have the movement
that catches your eye

00:18:11.490 --> 00:18:12.750
and draws you in.

00:18:12.750 --> 00:18:16.650
And this, again, connects
back to both the aspect

00:18:16.650 --> 00:18:19.500
of how are we going to draw
you in, but also, how are we

00:18:19.500 --> 00:18:21.300
going to make you stay?

00:18:21.300 --> 00:18:24.190
When people are
developing their bots,

00:18:24.190 --> 00:18:26.395
they're usually watching
hundreds of games

00:18:26.395 --> 00:18:28.020
over the course of
it, maybe thousands,

00:18:28.020 --> 00:18:31.470
as they're developing, seeing
other people's strategies

00:18:31.470 --> 00:18:32.290
and so on.

00:18:32.290 --> 00:18:33.790
And if it's not fun
to watch, you're

00:18:33.790 --> 00:18:36.030
not going to want
to participate.

00:18:36.030 --> 00:18:38.550
Last, it also helps with
the educational aspect.

00:18:38.550 --> 00:18:40.020
It makes it much--

00:18:40.020 --> 00:18:42.300
especially younger students
seem to care a lot more

00:18:42.300 --> 00:18:44.160
about this aspect,
that it's much more

00:18:44.160 --> 00:18:47.330
helpful in drawing them in.

00:18:47.330 --> 00:18:49.340
The game needs to be
difficult to solve.

00:18:49.340 --> 00:18:51.590
It should be evident that
tic-tac-toe would not

00:18:51.590 --> 00:18:55.460
make a great AI competition
game because it wouldn't

00:18:55.460 --> 00:18:57.720
last very long until
someone figured out

00:18:57.720 --> 00:19:00.030
the dominant strategy.

00:19:00.030 --> 00:19:03.260
And in this way, the
games that we work with

00:19:03.260 --> 00:19:05.310
need to not just be
difficult to solve,

00:19:05.310 --> 00:19:08.840
but they also need to be
beyond impossible to solve

00:19:08.840 --> 00:19:13.220
so there's always a lot of room
at the top for someone to do.

00:19:13.220 --> 00:19:15.230
You don't want a sort
of asymptotic behavior

00:19:15.230 --> 00:19:20.540
of you getting really close
to optimal strategies.

00:19:20.540 --> 00:19:22.880
One of the ways that we
achieve this, in part at least,

00:19:22.880 --> 00:19:28.670
is by having an action space
which is just enormous.

00:19:28.670 --> 00:19:33.430
The number of distinct move sets
for a standard Halite position

00:19:33.430 --> 00:19:36.500
in Halite I was on the
order of 10 to the 1,000th.

00:19:36.500 --> 00:19:38.780
Compare that with 300 for Go.

00:19:38.780 --> 00:19:40.890
Not 10 to the 300,
just 300, right?

00:19:40.890 --> 00:19:42.400
There's a lot--

00:19:42.400 --> 00:19:44.300
Monte Carlo is just
intractable when you have

00:19:44.300 --> 00:19:48.120
this sheer number of moves.

00:19:48.120 --> 00:19:50.520
And the final, most amorphous--

00:19:50.520 --> 00:19:52.250
I love this animation.

00:19:52.250 --> 00:19:57.424
The final, most amorphous
element of the game

00:19:57.424 --> 00:19:59.090
is that it needs to
be multidimensional.

00:19:59.090 --> 00:20:00.881
There needs to be a
lot of different things

00:20:00.881 --> 00:20:03.440
to do in the game so that
even if, as a developer,

00:20:03.440 --> 00:20:06.050
you decide, OK,
I've done all that

00:20:06.050 --> 00:20:08.870
I can do with, say, the combat
optimization element at the--

00:20:08.870 --> 00:20:10.292
I don't know what to do next.

00:20:10.292 --> 00:20:11.750
If that's all there
is to the game,

00:20:11.750 --> 00:20:14.540
you'll get frustrated
and probably stop.

00:20:14.540 --> 00:20:18.980
But we want you to say, oh,
but I have the next thing

00:20:18.980 --> 00:20:20.570
for planet selection.

00:20:20.570 --> 00:20:23.510
That's the next thing I need
to work on, or ship navigation,

00:20:23.510 --> 00:20:25.470
or who knows what.

00:20:25.470 --> 00:20:27.770
But as long as there's at
least five or six things

00:20:27.770 --> 00:20:31.620
to work with, it makes it more
interesting at the upper level.

00:20:31.620 --> 00:20:34.250
Then you've got differentiation
between the top players.

00:20:34.250 --> 00:20:36.700
So some bots are good at
different kinds of things.

00:20:36.700 --> 00:20:38.469
And also makes it
better for even

00:20:38.469 --> 00:20:40.760
the new developer, that
there's just something for them

00:20:40.760 --> 00:20:43.080
to always do.

00:20:43.080 --> 00:20:45.850
So how do we design these games?

00:20:45.850 --> 00:20:47.800
Well, the first thing
that we want to focus on

00:20:47.800 --> 00:20:50.180
is the fact that it's
really largely iterative.

00:20:50.180 --> 00:20:52.660
We don't know how to make these
games right the first time.

00:20:52.660 --> 00:20:55.930
Most games will satisfy one
or two of our requirements,

00:20:55.930 --> 00:20:59.020
but very few of them, three
or four, and almost no games

00:20:59.020 --> 00:21:01.120
satisfy everything that we want.

00:21:01.120 --> 00:21:03.040
What you see on the
left is the oldest image

00:21:03.040 --> 00:21:07.330
I have from my laptop
of Halite I development,

00:21:07.330 --> 00:21:10.360
and then on the right, the
actual finished Halite I game.

00:21:10.360 --> 00:21:14.200
And what I hope you see are both
the similarities between them--

00:21:14.200 --> 00:21:18.940
the grid like space and the
sort of blobish expansion with

00:21:18.940 --> 00:21:19.930
spaces between--

00:21:19.930 --> 00:21:21.370
but also the differences.

00:21:21.370 --> 00:21:23.600
The size of the map
is very different.

00:21:23.600 --> 00:21:27.820
The rules internally, which
you can't see, are different.

00:21:27.820 --> 00:21:29.740
And there's two or
three separate games

00:21:29.740 --> 00:21:32.260
that get us from the one
on the left to the right.

00:21:34.287 --> 00:21:34.870
MICHAEL: Yeah.

00:21:34.870 --> 00:21:38.260
The next thing we really try to
take to heart in cutting down

00:21:38.260 --> 00:21:40.870
these games once we've
decided on a base set of rules

00:21:40.870 --> 00:21:42.880
is we try, to the
best of our ability,

00:21:42.880 --> 00:21:45.010
to reduce the number of
unit types and constants

00:21:45.010 --> 00:21:46.030
in the game.

00:21:46.030 --> 00:21:48.934
So when you, just
by definition, have

00:21:48.934 --> 00:21:51.350
a lot of unit types in your
game, the length of your rules

00:21:51.350 --> 00:21:53.197
is going to be huge.

00:21:53.197 --> 00:21:55.030
Players are going to
have to memorize a tech

00:21:55.030 --> 00:21:58.120
tree or the abilities of the
soldier class versus the worker

00:21:58.120 --> 00:22:00.760
class, et cetera,
and that's fine.

00:22:00.760 --> 00:22:02.590
That appeals to a
certain demographic.

00:22:02.590 --> 00:22:07.772
But, for example, MIT Battle
Code has, for a decade plus,

00:22:07.772 --> 00:22:09.730
released games that take
advantage of this unit

00:22:09.730 --> 00:22:13.540
type classing to much success.

00:22:13.540 --> 00:22:16.000
And their demographic
really loves it.

00:22:16.000 --> 00:22:19.630
They enjoy scouring
through long page specs,

00:22:19.630 --> 00:22:21.620
trying to find that one
hole in the balancing

00:22:21.620 --> 00:22:24.797
so that they can write
the dominant strategy.

00:22:24.797 --> 00:22:27.130
But that's just not what we're
trying to do with Halite.

00:22:27.130 --> 00:22:30.580
We want Halite to be broadly
applicable to all programmers

00:22:30.580 --> 00:22:31.850
on the internet.

00:22:31.850 --> 00:22:33.880
We want them to be able
to write a bot after two

00:22:33.880 --> 00:22:38.320
minutes of looking at our API
and looking at our game rules.

00:22:38.320 --> 00:22:41.527
And so we really
try and reduce that.

00:22:41.527 --> 00:22:43.360
BEN: This also, I think,
ties into the theme

00:22:43.360 --> 00:22:45.070
of having players play
against each other

00:22:45.070 --> 00:22:46.540
rather than against the game.

00:22:46.540 --> 00:22:49.900
We feel that when it's about
reading through the spec

00:22:49.900 --> 00:22:51.970
and trying to just
understand what the game is

00:22:51.970 --> 00:22:58.930
and how to best use the flaws
for balancing of the game,

00:22:58.930 --> 00:23:00.640
then we really feel
like you're much more

00:23:00.640 --> 00:23:02.140
so playing against
the game than you

00:23:02.140 --> 00:23:03.850
are against the other players.

00:23:03.850 --> 00:23:05.350
The games are much
more interesting

00:23:05.350 --> 00:23:06.970
when you're looking at
how players interact

00:23:06.970 --> 00:23:09.095
with each other, which
we'll see a little bit later

00:23:09.095 --> 00:23:12.250
in our game theory section.

00:23:12.250 --> 00:23:14.770
The game is really just
a medium for players

00:23:14.770 --> 00:23:16.830
to actually interact and
learn from each other.

00:23:16.830 --> 00:23:17.330
Right?

00:23:17.330 --> 00:23:19.270
MICHAEL: Yeah.

00:23:19.270 --> 00:23:22.580
BEN: That brings us to the
point of emergent behaviors.

00:23:22.580 --> 00:23:26.710
This really is just a natural
thing that comes from the fact

00:23:26.710 --> 00:23:29.490
that we require the games to
be simple, difficult to solve,

00:23:29.490 --> 00:23:30.640
and multi-dimensional.

00:23:30.640 --> 00:23:34.120
So difficult to solve and
multi-dimensional basically

00:23:34.120 --> 00:23:37.090
means that you have to have lots
of distinct, complex behaviors

00:23:37.090 --> 00:23:39.520
that come out of it, but
yet, because it's simple,

00:23:39.520 --> 00:23:41.530
we can't write lots
of complex rules.

00:23:41.530 --> 00:23:44.710
So the only solution to this
is that we have simple rules,

00:23:44.710 --> 00:23:48.250
and from those simple rules
emerge these complex behaviors.

00:23:48.250 --> 00:23:50.170
What we have here,
what you're seeing

00:23:50.170 --> 00:23:55.382
is Conway's Game of Life,
a certain starting state.

00:23:55.382 --> 00:23:57.340
For those who don't know,
Conway's Game of Life

00:23:57.340 --> 00:23:59.296
is a cellular automata,
which basically

00:23:59.296 --> 00:24:00.670
means that the
state of each cell

00:24:00.670 --> 00:24:02.740
depends only on the
state of its neighbors.

00:24:02.740 --> 00:24:05.200
And the rules can be described
in like three bullet points.

00:24:05.200 --> 00:24:06.520
It's incredibly simple.

00:24:06.520 --> 00:24:08.200
But from these
simple rules come all

00:24:08.200 --> 00:24:11.470
these complicated behaviors,
that you have-- on the left,

00:24:11.470 --> 00:24:13.364
you see what are called
Glider Guns which

00:24:13.364 --> 00:24:15.280
are producing this
infinite stream of gliders.

00:24:15.280 --> 00:24:18.070
And then this whole
contraption is something

00:24:18.070 --> 00:24:20.380
called a Breeder, which
produces an infinite stream

00:24:20.380 --> 00:24:21.970
of these Glider Guns.

00:24:21.970 --> 00:24:24.370
People have even constructed
entire Turing Machines

00:24:24.370 --> 00:24:26.920
in Conway's Game of Life,
which shows you just the level

00:24:26.920 --> 00:24:30.530
complexity associated with it.

00:24:30.530 --> 00:24:32.040
So we were really
inspired by that.

00:24:32.040 --> 00:24:36.340
We wanted to replicate that
kind of emergence with Halite I.

00:24:36.340 --> 00:24:39.250
But there's a second
side of this coin, which

00:24:39.250 --> 00:24:41.800
is that when the behaviors
that you're trying to design

00:24:41.800 --> 00:24:44.470
are emergent, by definition,
you can't understand them

00:24:44.470 --> 00:24:47.380
just by breaking it down
to the constituent pieces.

00:24:47.380 --> 00:24:50.830
So as far as we know,
the only solution to this

00:24:50.830 --> 00:24:51.880
is play testing.

00:24:51.880 --> 00:24:54.490
We make games, and we see
what are the kinds of things

00:24:54.490 --> 00:24:56.020
that we expect to
be effective are,

00:24:56.020 --> 00:24:59.509
the things that shouldn't be
aren't, and make that all work.

00:24:59.509 --> 00:25:01.300
If anyone knows a better
way of doing this,

00:25:01.300 --> 00:25:05.050
please come see me
after the talk finishes.

00:25:05.050 --> 00:25:08.380
I would love to know.

00:25:08.380 --> 00:25:13.360
So that will take us, finally,
to targeted statistics.

00:25:13.360 --> 00:25:15.670
This is, I think, just
more of a subpoint of this.

00:25:15.670 --> 00:25:17.729
Often with these games,
when we're trying--

00:25:17.729 --> 00:25:19.270
we have most of the
game figured out.

00:25:19.270 --> 00:25:21.820
We want to just tune
certain parameters of it,

00:25:21.820 --> 00:25:24.010
make it as interesting
as possible.

00:25:24.010 --> 00:25:25.720
We'll sometimes also
collect statistics

00:25:25.720 --> 00:25:27.730
that can be used to better
understand the game.

00:25:27.730 --> 00:25:29.410
So what you're
seeing right here,

00:25:29.410 --> 00:25:33.470
we've co-opted from
the field of economics

00:25:33.470 --> 00:25:35.740
the Gini coefficient, which
is a statistical measure

00:25:35.740 --> 00:25:36.707
of inequality.

00:25:36.707 --> 00:25:38.290
We've applied it to
Halite maps to try

00:25:38.290 --> 00:25:41.050
to figure out how production
on Halite maps is distributed.

00:25:44.384 --> 00:25:46.800
This allows us to produce this
distribution that basically

00:25:46.800 --> 00:25:49.230
shows us that most
maps have roughly

00:25:49.230 --> 00:25:52.350
this level of distribution
of production through them,

00:25:52.350 --> 00:25:54.600
but that there's also
this kind of variance.

00:25:54.600 --> 00:25:56.700
To us, this is a good
kind of distribution,

00:25:56.700 --> 00:25:58.930
because it shows
that, for a beginner,

00:25:58.930 --> 00:26:01.800
you can mostly ignore the
outside of that distribution.

00:26:01.800 --> 00:26:04.510
You can say most games I'm
going to be playing roughly

00:26:04.510 --> 00:26:06.900
work with this kind of
math, this rough level

00:26:06.900 --> 00:26:09.460
of distribution.

00:26:09.460 --> 00:26:12.540
But that also, on
the outside, there's

00:26:12.540 --> 00:26:14.400
lots of different
variety of maps

00:26:14.400 --> 00:26:16.075
that happen less
frequently, and that

00:26:16.075 --> 00:26:17.700
makes for some really
interesting games

00:26:17.700 --> 00:26:18.670
every once and a while.

00:26:18.670 --> 00:26:21.360
So that allows us to help
verify that the games that we're

00:26:21.360 --> 00:26:22.970
making really are interesting.

00:26:22.970 --> 00:26:24.120
MICHAEL: Yeah.

00:26:24.120 --> 00:26:25.500
So now we're going to--

00:26:25.500 --> 00:26:27.130
BEN: Click.

00:26:27.130 --> 00:26:28.950
MICHAEL: Two clickers.

00:26:28.950 --> 00:26:31.170
So now we're going to
talk in broad categories

00:26:31.170 --> 00:26:34.110
about the solutions, the
interesting solutions

00:26:34.110 --> 00:26:37.680
that our contestants made
for Halite I and Halite II.

00:26:37.680 --> 00:26:41.010
I think this is a good point
to mention that, Ben and I,

00:26:41.010 --> 00:26:43.920
we think that AI encompasses
a lot more than deep learning.

00:26:43.920 --> 00:26:45.930
There are a lot more
interesting techniques

00:26:45.930 --> 00:26:49.530
such as heuristic-based
models and using game theory

00:26:49.530 --> 00:26:53.190
to solve the sorts of
problems that deep learning is

00:26:53.190 --> 00:26:54.352
trying to solve.

00:26:54.352 --> 00:26:54.852
BEN: Right.

00:26:54.852 --> 00:26:57.210
And for the record,
we love deep learning.

00:26:57.210 --> 00:26:58.799
There's a lot of
amazing research

00:26:58.799 --> 00:27:00.090
that's come out recently in it.

00:27:00.090 --> 00:27:01.920
We just think--
tool in the toolbox.

00:27:01.920 --> 00:27:06.090
A lot of our users are very
inexperienced at programming.

00:27:06.090 --> 00:27:08.160
The vast majority of them
have under three years

00:27:08.160 --> 00:27:09.340
of experience.

00:27:09.340 --> 00:27:11.700
So we have to make
sure that we support

00:27:11.700 --> 00:27:13.950
all sorts of different
users, not just those that

00:27:13.950 --> 00:27:15.158
are interested in deep learn.

00:27:15.158 --> 00:27:15.900
MICHAEL: Yeah.

00:27:15.900 --> 00:27:19.800
And so the most prevalent
category of solutions

00:27:19.800 --> 00:27:22.560
to Halite I and Halite II is
just simply the heuristic bot.

00:27:22.560 --> 00:27:24.450
And all that a
heuristic bot is is it's

00:27:24.450 --> 00:27:27.780
just hand-coded logic,
hand-coded strategies

00:27:27.780 --> 00:27:29.880
for playing the games of
Halite I and Halite II.

00:27:29.880 --> 00:27:31.650
And so one example
of a heuristic

00:27:31.650 --> 00:27:33.960
is just go to the
highest production zone,

00:27:33.960 --> 00:27:35.680
which is actually shown here.

00:27:35.680 --> 00:27:38.010
So the production that
Ben referenced before when

00:27:38.010 --> 00:27:40.731
talking about the map Gini
coefficient is shown by--

00:27:40.731 --> 00:27:42.480
I don't know how well
you can see it here,

00:27:42.480 --> 00:27:44.880
but it's the back shading
on this visualizer.

00:27:44.880 --> 00:27:47.640
So you see that the area that
this yellow player is going to

00:27:47.640 --> 00:27:49.050
is a high production zone.

00:27:49.050 --> 00:27:51.300
And so at the start
of the game of Halite,

00:27:51.300 --> 00:27:53.220
it tunnels to that
zone and then starts

00:27:53.220 --> 00:27:55.930
to expand so it can get a
jump on its production rates.

00:27:55.930 --> 00:27:58.020
And heuristic bots
are interesting to us

00:27:58.020 --> 00:27:58.720
for two reasons.

00:27:58.720 --> 00:28:00.194
One, they're really democratic.

00:28:00.194 --> 00:28:01.110
They're really simple.

00:28:01.110 --> 00:28:03.180
Anybody with moderate
programming skills

00:28:03.180 --> 00:28:04.110
can write these bots.

00:28:04.110 --> 00:28:05.490
It's just simple logic.

00:28:05.490 --> 00:28:08.556
And then the second point is,
it requires humans to actually

00:28:08.556 --> 00:28:10.680
think through the strategy,
and it's really in line

00:28:10.680 --> 00:28:16.260
with our human plus machine
way of constructing games.

00:28:16.260 --> 00:28:18.780
When you use something
like Monte Carlo trees

00:28:18.780 --> 00:28:21.499
that are generally
applicable to all games,

00:28:21.499 --> 00:28:23.790
you're not thinking through
the specifics of that game.

00:28:23.790 --> 00:28:26.100
You're just applying
that hammer, that tool

00:28:26.100 --> 00:28:27.806
in the toolbox, to the game.

00:28:27.806 --> 00:28:29.430
And the same is true
for deep learning.

00:28:29.430 --> 00:28:31.620
But with heuristics
bots, you really

00:28:31.620 --> 00:28:33.360
are combining the
power of humans

00:28:33.360 --> 00:28:34.736
and the power of machines.

00:28:34.736 --> 00:28:37.110
BEN: This also, I think, ties
into the educational aspect

00:28:37.110 --> 00:28:38.760
of it, that by
forcing you to think

00:28:38.760 --> 00:28:42.240
through the game and
your logic and what

00:28:42.240 --> 00:28:45.287
the effects of your logic
will be on how your bot plays

00:28:45.287 --> 00:28:47.370
the game, we think this
is a really important step

00:28:47.370 --> 00:28:51.810
in teaching lots of younger
programmers how to get better.

00:28:51.810 --> 00:28:54.030
This ability to
really visually see

00:28:54.030 --> 00:28:56.950
how your changes affect
your bot and the behavior

00:28:56.950 --> 00:28:59.280
is an incredibly
valuable tool in learning

00:28:59.280 --> 00:29:01.500
to do so even more effectively.

00:29:01.500 --> 00:29:02.520
MICHAEL: Yeah.

00:29:02.520 --> 00:29:06.210
And then this is a more
complicated heuristic schema

00:29:06.210 --> 00:29:07.324
for Halite II.

00:29:07.324 --> 00:29:09.240
So we aren't going to
go into too much detail.

00:29:09.240 --> 00:29:11.737
This was from a top 100
player in the competition.

00:29:11.737 --> 00:29:13.320
And broadly, what
he did is every turn

00:29:13.320 --> 00:29:15.990
he computed a gradient,
which is shown here in 3D

00:29:15.990 --> 00:29:17.340
with the hills and the valleys.

00:29:17.340 --> 00:29:18.930
And what he did for
each of his ships

00:29:18.930 --> 00:29:20.550
is he looked at the
gradient, and he

00:29:20.550 --> 00:29:22.883
had the ship go in a direction
that a ball bearing would

00:29:22.883 --> 00:29:25.290
go if it was just rolling
down these hills, right?

00:29:25.290 --> 00:29:28.350
And the whole strategy
of this schema

00:29:28.350 --> 00:29:31.260
is encoded in a few
setup parameters,

00:29:31.260 --> 00:29:33.210
and these parameters
are just how much

00:29:33.210 --> 00:29:36.854
in terms of downhill-ness
or uphill-ness.

00:29:36.854 --> 00:29:38.770
Various game objects
will affect the gradient.

00:29:38.770 --> 00:29:41.880
So for example, you want
to stay away a little bit

00:29:41.880 --> 00:29:44.040
from your own ship, your
friendly ships in Halite

00:29:44.040 --> 00:29:46.650
II, because if you collide
with your own ships,

00:29:46.650 --> 00:29:48.120
they're eliminated, right?

00:29:48.120 --> 00:29:51.860
And so they'll have a bit of an
uphill effect on the gradient.

00:29:51.860 --> 00:29:53.715
BEN: Yeah.

00:29:53.715 --> 00:29:56.160
We do have lots of players
that do utilize machine

00:29:56.160 --> 00:29:58.150
learning and deep learning.

00:29:58.150 --> 00:30:00.660
So what you see here
is a diagram of a CNN

00:30:00.660 --> 00:30:03.240
that was used to play
Halite I to great effect.

00:30:03.240 --> 00:30:06.120
This was from user jstaker7.

00:30:06.120 --> 00:30:09.150
So certain kinds of
deep learning methods

00:30:09.150 --> 00:30:12.540
don't work particularly well
with Halite, namely the AlphaGo

00:30:12.540 --> 00:30:16.240
style and Monte Carlo methods,
because the space is so large.

00:30:16.240 --> 00:30:17.190
But lots of them do.

00:30:17.190 --> 00:30:19.080
In this case, this
was simply trained

00:30:19.080 --> 00:30:22.110
on the moves of the
top couple of users

00:30:22.110 --> 00:30:25.671
and got handily into the top
20 of the competition out

00:30:25.671 --> 00:30:26.670
of thousands of players.

00:30:26.670 --> 00:30:28.650
So that's pretty good, we think.

00:30:28.650 --> 00:30:31.830
We've also had players
used reinforcement learning

00:30:31.830 --> 00:30:35.400
and policy gradients on
parametrized non-deep models.

00:30:35.400 --> 00:30:37.530
That's also been used
to pretty good effect--

00:30:37.530 --> 00:30:39.630
top 100.

00:30:39.630 --> 00:30:40.710
Yeah.

00:30:40.710 --> 00:30:44.157
And we are excited
for what's going

00:30:44.157 --> 00:30:46.240
to continue to happen,
especially with Halite III.

00:30:46.240 --> 00:30:48.160
I don't say too much,
except that we think

00:30:48.160 --> 00:30:49.450
that there's a lot of promise.

00:30:49.450 --> 00:30:51.670
We've been exploring
it personally

00:30:51.670 --> 00:30:55.850
for what can be done with Halite
III in the deep learning space.

00:30:55.850 --> 00:31:01.870
MICHAEL: And then finally, one
of the nice things about Halite

00:31:01.870 --> 00:31:04.780
is that, since we try and build
in this multi-dimensionality,

00:31:04.780 --> 00:31:07.810
there is both the element of
local play at the piece level,

00:31:07.810 --> 00:31:11.590
and also there is definitely
an important level of metagame,

00:31:11.590 --> 00:31:12.550
of metaplay.

00:31:12.550 --> 00:31:14.500
And so we saw this in
Halite I, actually.

00:31:14.500 --> 00:31:17.110
At the end of the competition,
a very important emergent

00:31:17.110 --> 00:31:18.879
behavior in the
metagame was something

00:31:18.879 --> 00:31:20.170
called the Non-Aggression Pact.

00:31:20.170 --> 00:31:22.570
And it was basically
just an implicit alliance

00:31:22.570 --> 00:31:25.240
between every player in a game.

00:31:25.240 --> 00:31:27.580
If you enter the
alliance, the logic

00:31:27.580 --> 00:31:29.230
that you encode in
your bot is that you

00:31:29.230 --> 00:31:32.410
won't attack any other player
until they've attacked you.

00:31:32.410 --> 00:31:35.560
And so, say if five out of
the six players in a game

00:31:35.560 --> 00:31:38.170
put this logic in their bot,
that last sixth player is

00:31:38.170 --> 00:31:41.140
going to be attacked
by all five first.

00:31:41.140 --> 00:31:43.370
And so because of
this, all the players

00:31:43.370 --> 00:31:46.887
at the top of our leaderboard
in the last few days of the game

00:31:46.887 --> 00:31:48.220
encoded this logic in their bot.

00:31:48.220 --> 00:31:49.330
It really exploded then.

00:31:49.330 --> 00:31:49.830
BEN: Yeah.

00:31:49.830 --> 00:31:52.510
It was very unexpected by us.

00:31:52.510 --> 00:31:54.520
It's just a huge
shift in the rankings

00:31:54.520 --> 00:31:58.840
in the last couple of
days, especially including

00:31:58.840 --> 00:32:03.220
our very top user, who
probably was significantly

00:32:03.220 --> 00:32:05.890
influenced by his incredibly
effective handling

00:32:05.890 --> 00:32:07.530
of this whole metagame.

00:32:07.530 --> 00:32:08.630
MICHAEL: Yes.

00:32:08.630 --> 00:32:10.121
BEN: Yeah.

00:32:10.121 --> 00:32:13.130
So then finally, we're going
to talk about our competition

00:32:13.130 --> 00:32:13.630
principles.

00:32:13.630 --> 00:32:14.864
MICHAEL: Yeah.

00:32:14.864 --> 00:32:16.780
These are the principles
we take to heart when

00:32:16.780 --> 00:32:19.750
we're building the competition
around the Halite games, so

00:32:19.750 --> 00:32:21.350
stuff like our
learning resources,

00:32:21.350 --> 00:32:24.690
our website, our forums,
our tooling, et cetera.

00:32:24.690 --> 00:32:26.800
And so the most important
of these principles

00:32:26.800 --> 00:32:29.410
is that they need to be very
beginner-friendly but also

00:32:29.410 --> 00:32:30.160
powerful.

00:32:30.160 --> 00:32:32.260
So like Ben said
earlier, we're dealing

00:32:32.260 --> 00:32:33.430
with people on the internet.

00:32:33.430 --> 00:32:36.040
They have minute-long
attention spans.

00:32:36.040 --> 00:32:37.780
They need to be
interested by Halite

00:32:37.780 --> 00:32:40.884
after just reading the
game rules for a paragraph

00:32:40.884 --> 00:32:42.300
and looking at our
tooling, right?

00:32:42.300 --> 00:32:44.290
And so one way in
which we make it

00:32:44.290 --> 00:32:46.120
very frictionless
to onboard people

00:32:46.120 --> 00:32:48.520
is we support 20 plus languages.

00:32:48.520 --> 00:32:51.190
Most of these are
contributed by the community.

00:32:51.190 --> 00:32:54.970
But that means that, if you're
a beginner and you like Python,

00:32:54.970 --> 00:32:56.470
you can jump into
the game, but also

00:32:56.470 --> 00:32:58.240
if you're a Rust
enthusiast, you don't

00:32:58.240 --> 00:33:01.960
have to deal with the
annoyance of Java.

00:33:01.960 --> 00:33:04.570
But also, we take this to
heart with her other resources.

00:33:04.570 --> 00:33:06.910
For Halite III, we're
working on a web

00:33:06.910 --> 00:33:10.330
ID for users who are not
competent in the command

00:33:10.330 --> 00:33:13.090
line and also some more
interactive tutorial

00:33:13.090 --> 00:33:14.290
experiences.

00:33:14.290 --> 00:33:16.660
And then the second
thing we take to heart

00:33:16.660 --> 00:33:20.660
is the competition is really
real time to its core.

00:33:20.660 --> 00:33:23.380
So we've already mentioned
the continuous leaderboard

00:33:23.380 --> 00:33:25.420
that is central to how
Halite works, and we've

00:33:25.420 --> 00:33:27.880
heard from users that
that is one of the best

00:33:27.880 --> 00:33:29.650
things about the competition.

00:33:29.650 --> 00:33:32.860
For example, that third
user you see there, shummie,

00:33:32.860 --> 00:33:36.820
is a very enthusiastic All-State
actuary who submitted 600 bot

00:33:36.820 --> 00:33:38.890
updates to the competition.

00:33:38.890 --> 00:33:41.350
And our median level of bot
submission was very high.

00:33:41.350 --> 00:33:44.140
I think it was something
around 20-something for Halite

00:33:44.140 --> 00:33:46.520
I was the median level.

00:33:46.520 --> 00:33:48.612
So yeah, the leaderboard
got a lot of use

00:33:48.612 --> 00:33:50.820
because you can make just
a small change to your bot,

00:33:50.820 --> 00:33:52.600
submit it, and within
a couple of minutes

00:33:52.600 --> 00:33:55.120
you're seeing that new bot
playing games against everybody

00:33:55.120 --> 00:33:56.170
else on the leaderboard.

00:33:56.170 --> 00:33:57.760
It's very immediate feedback.

00:33:57.760 --> 00:34:01.000
And then we also need to
build in collaborative spaces

00:34:01.000 --> 00:34:05.230
for users online, because often
other bot developers can help

00:34:05.230 --> 00:34:07.060
users better than
we can help them,

00:34:07.060 --> 00:34:09.699
because they've actually had the
annoyances, the frustrations,

00:34:09.699 --> 00:34:11.969
the joys of working
on Halite, and we're

00:34:11.969 --> 00:34:13.510
coming into it with
a lot of existing

00:34:13.510 --> 00:34:16.460
knowledge about the competition
because we built it.

00:34:16.460 --> 00:34:19.300
And so one way in
which we do this is we

00:34:19.300 --> 00:34:23.040
have a discourse forum with
some nice theming shown here.

00:34:23.040 --> 00:34:27.489
This saw a lot of use,
100-reply-long threads

00:34:27.489 --> 00:34:30.760
about everything from bot
strategies to bug reports

00:34:30.760 --> 00:34:35.620
to new tools that the
community has developed.

00:34:35.620 --> 00:34:38.409
Somebody developed,
a third party user,

00:34:38.409 --> 00:34:42.326
a very popular visualizer for
Halite II that saw a lot of use

00:34:42.326 --> 00:34:42.909
on the forums.

00:34:42.909 --> 00:34:44.450
And the second thing
we do is we also

00:34:44.450 --> 00:34:46.870
have a discourse chat
that lots of players used.

00:34:46.870 --> 00:34:49.739
And then finally,
what we're dealing

00:34:49.739 --> 00:34:52.030
with on the back end of
Halite, which we haven't really

00:34:52.030 --> 00:34:53.699
touched on a lot in
this presentation,

00:34:53.699 --> 00:34:56.860
but we're about to, is kind
of a hard engineering problem,

00:34:56.860 --> 00:35:02.470
because for Halite II,
we had about 6,500 users,

00:35:02.470 --> 00:35:05.360
and we need to rank all of
these users very quickly.

00:35:05.360 --> 00:35:08.740
And so we run tens of millions
of games of the Halite

00:35:08.740 --> 00:35:11.350
on our back end to rank all
these users over the lifetime

00:35:11.350 --> 00:35:12.470
of the competition.

00:35:12.470 --> 00:35:14.210
And so this is hard
for two reasons.

00:35:14.210 --> 00:35:17.980
One, we're running untrusted
user code on our servers,

00:35:17.980 --> 00:35:19.670
so security is a big problem.

00:35:19.670 --> 00:35:22.000
And then also, this
needs to be scaled.

00:35:22.000 --> 00:35:23.405
We're going from
running no games

00:35:23.405 --> 00:35:25.780
at the start of the competition
to many millions of games

00:35:25.780 --> 00:35:27.446
in a week at the end
of the competition,

00:35:27.446 --> 00:35:30.214
so we need our infrastructure
to scale up seamlessly.

00:35:30.214 --> 00:35:31.630
And so Jacques,
our lead engineer,

00:35:31.630 --> 00:35:34.516
is here to talk about that.

00:35:34.516 --> 00:35:36.886
JACQUES: Thank you.

00:35:36.886 --> 00:35:37.834
Thank you.

00:35:37.834 --> 00:35:40.780
So as-- Hi.

00:35:40.780 --> 00:35:41.860
Sorry.

00:35:41.860 --> 00:35:45.430
So, as Ben and Michael said,
it is a very, very difficult

00:35:45.430 --> 00:35:47.950
problem to solve, and for a
very, very difficult problem,

00:35:47.950 --> 00:35:50.870
we require good architecture.

00:35:50.870 --> 00:35:53.330
So that's how we use Google.

00:35:53.330 --> 00:35:55.870
So Google has in
this way actually

00:35:55.870 --> 00:35:58.180
granted us the ability to
solve all of these problems

00:35:58.180 --> 00:36:01.817
in a very simple way,
almost plug and play.

00:36:01.817 --> 00:36:03.400
And so right now I'm
going to walk you

00:36:03.400 --> 00:36:06.602
through all the problems, all
the challenges that we had,

00:36:06.602 --> 00:36:08.560
and how we solved them,
and how Google actually

00:36:08.560 --> 00:36:11.380
helped us solve them.

00:36:11.380 --> 00:36:14.229
First of all, games
are ripe with cheating.

00:36:14.229 --> 00:36:16.270
The greater the competition,
the greater the want

00:36:16.270 --> 00:36:17.590
for users to cheat.

00:36:17.590 --> 00:36:19.540
And we store quite
a large number

00:36:19.540 --> 00:36:21.190
of personally
identifiable information

00:36:21.190 --> 00:36:24.290
here, including user
e-mails, among other things.

00:36:24.290 --> 00:36:25.990
And those two
factors mean we need

00:36:25.990 --> 00:36:28.495
a lot of security to
ensure malicious activity

00:36:28.495 --> 00:36:30.430
is at a minimum.

00:36:30.430 --> 00:36:33.280
And of course, we also
run a large, large amount

00:36:33.280 --> 00:36:35.390
of arbitrary code,
as was mentioned.

00:36:35.390 --> 00:36:39.310
We have 6,000 users with a total
of over 50,000 bots submitted

00:36:39.310 --> 00:36:43.870
in over 25 languages
and lots of frameworks,

00:36:43.870 --> 00:36:47.750
and that's a lot of
untrusted of code.

00:36:47.750 --> 00:36:49.960
And, as I said, a lot of
personally identifiable

00:36:49.960 --> 00:36:52.580
information, and all of
that adds to the problem.

00:36:52.580 --> 00:36:55.450
Secondly, variable
ranking algorithms

00:36:55.450 --> 00:36:58.530
need to play a lot of games
before fair convergence,

00:36:58.530 --> 00:37:01.040
and especially if
there's a lot of users,

00:37:01.040 --> 00:37:03.100
this increases
exponentially, which

00:37:03.100 --> 00:37:06.610
requires much more
compute in many more games

00:37:06.610 --> 00:37:07.990
and with a time.

00:37:07.990 --> 00:37:10.570
And the outcome of this is
either general frustration

00:37:10.570 --> 00:37:15.100
for users or large costs to the
administrators, and many times

00:37:15.100 --> 00:37:16.630
both.

00:37:16.630 --> 00:37:19.240
Regionality is
also a big problem.

00:37:19.240 --> 00:37:21.280
A competition encompassing
over 100 countries

00:37:21.280 --> 00:37:24.400
worldwide creates
some complexity.

00:37:24.400 --> 00:37:29.590
Explicitly, how do we provide
equally fast responses

00:37:29.590 --> 00:37:31.240
to players all over the globe?

00:37:31.240 --> 00:37:33.400
Though we don't require
sub-millisecond responses

00:37:33.400 --> 00:37:36.140
like a lot of multiplayer
games require,

00:37:36.140 --> 00:37:38.140
we still need to do things
with some expediency,

00:37:38.140 --> 00:37:41.140
otherwise users will be upset.

00:37:41.140 --> 00:37:42.880
Reliability is a
very common problem,

00:37:42.880 --> 00:37:44.337
but it's a problem nonetheless.

00:37:44.337 --> 00:37:46.420
How do you make sure the
disturbances are minimal?

00:37:46.420 --> 00:37:49.480
Users generally shy away from
anything, especially games,

00:37:49.480 --> 00:37:50.260
they can't access.

00:37:50.260 --> 00:37:52.500
And it doesn't matter
how fun you make a game.

00:37:52.500 --> 00:37:55.130
If you can't play it, no
one's going to play it.

00:37:55.130 --> 00:37:58.750
So for that, we need to
minimize the downtime.

00:37:58.750 --> 00:38:00.411
AI is another,
well, quote unquote,

00:38:00.411 --> 00:38:01.660
problem that we want to solve.

00:38:01.660 --> 00:38:04.370
We want to make this
game amenable to AI.

00:38:04.370 --> 00:38:06.370
And for games to be
variably solvable,

00:38:06.370 --> 00:38:10.780
we need to provide both data and
tools related to the problem.

00:38:10.780 --> 00:38:13.870
And especially as the data
increases, which in our case

00:38:13.870 --> 00:38:17.330
includes megabyte replays from
over 25 million games, which

00:38:17.330 --> 00:38:19.120
you can see is a
lot, the problem

00:38:19.120 --> 00:38:22.010
becomes increasingly
more difficult.

00:38:22.010 --> 00:38:23.060
So how do we solve them?

00:38:23.060 --> 00:38:25.310
We have two types of
solutions we have here,

00:38:25.310 --> 00:38:28.250
ones that we technically got for
free by using Google, and ones

00:38:28.250 --> 00:38:29.950
that we had to build in house.

00:38:29.950 --> 00:38:32.470
So I'm going to separate
them in all of the solutions.

00:38:32.470 --> 00:38:35.540
First off, in terms of
security, through GCP

00:38:35.540 --> 00:38:37.630
we got good containerization,
so we didn't

00:38:37.630 --> 00:38:40.000
have to worry about that.

00:38:40.000 --> 00:38:43.600
We managed to close all
internet routes going outside

00:38:43.600 --> 00:38:46.360
of all our machines that
were running untrusted code,

00:38:46.360 --> 00:38:48.890
so people couldn't do bad things
like, say, bitcoin mining,

00:38:48.890 --> 00:38:50.830
in our machines.

00:38:50.830 --> 00:38:53.469
We placed a good
number of firewalls

00:38:53.469 --> 00:38:55.510
to make sure people just
weren't doing bad things

00:38:55.510 --> 00:38:57.010
and talking to
machines they weren't

00:38:57.010 --> 00:38:58.540
supposed to be talking to.

00:38:58.540 --> 00:39:01.960
We created a large amount
of database in GCS isolation

00:39:01.960 --> 00:39:05.320
so that only some machines
could talk to them.

00:39:05.320 --> 00:39:07.600
And, of course, we used the
great VPC service controls

00:39:07.600 --> 00:39:10.570
that are used widely that
really allow us to just talk

00:39:10.570 --> 00:39:13.150
to the machines that we
want to talk and not have

00:39:13.150 --> 00:39:15.310
any indirectness towards that.

00:39:15.310 --> 00:39:17.440
And, of course, in our
in-house solutions,

00:39:17.440 --> 00:39:20.500
we had a vast number of system
level defenses including

00:39:20.500 --> 00:39:23.590
cgroups, IP tables,
among other things,

00:39:23.590 --> 00:39:26.500
that allows us to really
isolate everything.

00:39:26.500 --> 00:39:29.041
We had bot anonymization,
so if you're playing,

00:39:29.041 --> 00:39:31.040
you didn't really know
what the other bots were,

00:39:31.040 --> 00:39:33.640
so you couldn't exploit
anything related to that.

00:39:33.640 --> 00:39:36.340
And we created score penalties,
like everything else.

00:39:36.340 --> 00:39:39.100
I mean, you can think of that
as, really, an AI problem.

00:39:39.100 --> 00:39:42.190
As people do malicious
things, you penalize them,

00:39:42.190 --> 00:39:43.810
and they stop doing
malicious things.

00:39:43.810 --> 00:39:45.190
So it's a type of
reinforcement learning

00:39:45.190 --> 00:39:46.148
that we gave our users.

00:39:48.680 --> 00:39:52.370
So for score
convergence, in GCP,

00:39:52.370 --> 00:39:53.770
we had auto-scaling
groups, which

00:39:53.770 --> 00:39:55.410
were great because
suddenly, based

00:39:55.410 --> 00:39:57.160
on the number of users,
just automatically

00:39:57.160 --> 00:39:58.559
became bigger or smaller.

00:39:58.559 --> 00:40:00.100
And of course, that
lowered the cost.

00:40:00.100 --> 00:40:02.320
And what lowered the costs
even more significantly

00:40:02.320 --> 00:40:03.210
are preemptibles.

00:40:03.210 --> 00:40:05.110
Preemptibles have
made it insanely cheap

00:40:05.110 --> 00:40:08.350
for us to run a lot of things
and get very quick convergence.

00:40:08.350 --> 00:40:11.500
In house, we had effective
scoring algorithms.

00:40:11.500 --> 00:40:13.810
We used the best of the best
to make sure it was fast,

00:40:13.810 --> 00:40:16.510
and we tuned it to the
best of our abilities

00:40:16.510 --> 00:40:18.970
to make sure it was really fast.

00:40:18.970 --> 00:40:23.810
In terms of regionality, this
was all on Google, thankfully.

00:40:23.810 --> 00:40:26.320
Reliable regionality
toggle switch

00:40:26.320 --> 00:40:29.140
is one of their best features.

00:40:29.140 --> 00:40:31.480
So we really just used that.

00:40:31.480 --> 00:40:36.230
And plug and play caching
and CDNs for website and data

00:40:36.230 --> 00:40:39.920
respectively significantly
simplify the problem.

00:40:39.920 --> 00:40:41.799
Google has its
famous mini nines,

00:40:41.799 --> 00:40:44.090
which you can see based on
even the room you have here,

00:40:44.090 --> 00:40:47.180
the Five Nines Room,
which makes the service

00:40:47.180 --> 00:40:49.790
portion of this a non-problem.

00:40:49.790 --> 00:40:54.230
However, the use of preemptibles
adds a further complexity.

00:40:54.230 --> 00:40:57.410
Machines now can and will
go down at any point,

00:40:57.410 --> 00:41:00.350
making our code redundant,
more specifically, duplicating

00:41:00.350 --> 00:41:04.470
executions and services,
saving results, doing backups,

00:41:04.470 --> 00:41:07.370
using health checks,
automatic restarts or creates.

00:41:07.370 --> 00:41:10.050
And leveraging
fault-tolerant algorithms

00:41:10.050 --> 00:41:14.810
is not a new solution, but
it's the key to reliability.

00:41:14.810 --> 00:41:17.270
The solution, of course,
translates beyond preemptibles,

00:41:17.270 --> 00:41:19.550
and ensures that even in
a rather faulty execution,

00:41:19.550 --> 00:41:23.570
Byzantine failures
will be resilient.

00:41:23.570 --> 00:41:26.120
Ultimately, Google is
very, very well-geared

00:41:26.120 --> 00:41:27.410
to solving AI problems.

00:41:27.410 --> 00:41:30.170
As you may have heard
in other sessions,

00:41:30.170 --> 00:41:32.450
that's what they're amazing at.

00:41:32.450 --> 00:41:37.610
By utilizing very powerful GPUs
and storing everything in GCS,

00:41:37.610 --> 00:41:41.157
we significantly improve the
process for all the users.

00:41:41.157 --> 00:41:42.740
For everything else,
we wrote a number

00:41:42.740 --> 00:41:44.450
of training tools in
Python to simplify

00:41:44.450 --> 00:41:48.770
the process in both interacting
with GCP and Halite directly,

00:41:48.770 --> 00:41:53.900
which ultimately gives us this
picture, which is specifically

00:41:53.900 --> 00:41:56.120
what our end picture was.

00:41:56.120 --> 00:41:58.520
We had CDN serving all
of the important data.

00:41:58.520 --> 00:42:01.670
Inside of our VPC, which was
well-protected, as I mentioned,

00:42:01.670 --> 00:42:03.422
we had three types
of machines, all

00:42:03.422 --> 00:42:04.880
done by management
disk groups that

00:42:04.880 --> 00:42:07.880
grew or decreased as required.

00:42:07.880 --> 00:42:10.190
In this sense, we had the
website, which literally just

00:42:10.190 --> 00:42:11.090
served the website.

00:42:11.090 --> 00:42:14.000
We had the coordinators,
which were the key API

00:42:14.000 --> 00:42:18.170
and managed to send games
for the workers to play.

00:42:18.170 --> 00:42:20.270
The workers could only
talk to the coordinators.

00:42:20.270 --> 00:42:22.250
Meanwhile, the coordinators
were the liaison,

00:42:22.250 --> 00:42:25.850
the mediator in between
GCS and Cloud SQL,

00:42:25.850 --> 00:42:28.520
where we really stored all
that we needed to store.

00:42:28.520 --> 00:42:31.990
And through that, we got
our complete picture.

00:42:31.990 --> 00:42:35.020
And now, for why Halite,
I bring it back to Elsie.

00:42:39.990 --> 00:42:41.320
ELSIE KENYON: All right.

00:42:41.320 --> 00:42:46.840
So why, again, do we
care about Halite?

00:42:46.840 --> 00:42:52.380
Let's go back to what Ben
was saying about game's role

00:42:52.380 --> 00:42:55.720
in playing, in learning, right?

00:42:55.720 --> 00:43:01.310
We learn through play,
but competitions also

00:43:01.310 --> 00:43:03.810
provide an opportunity
to learn from each other.

00:43:03.810 --> 00:43:06.090
They're not just about winning.

00:43:06.090 --> 00:43:09.560
They allow us to educate
each other through play,

00:43:09.560 --> 00:43:12.410
any age, any experience level.

00:43:12.410 --> 00:43:15.500
In Halite, we've heard
examples of how players

00:43:15.500 --> 00:43:17.300
learn by example of other bots.

00:43:17.300 --> 00:43:20.660
There were trends in
different strategies.

00:43:20.660 --> 00:43:23.060
Players also
contributed tutorials

00:43:23.060 --> 00:43:26.300
and bought postmortems to
share what they had learned,

00:43:26.300 --> 00:43:28.610
and also expressed
their creativity

00:43:28.610 --> 00:43:33.590
by finding new opportunities for
new ways to play with Halite--

00:43:33.590 --> 00:43:39.190
for example, writing their
names with their bots.

00:43:39.190 --> 00:43:42.190
Technology really
enables this connectivity

00:43:42.190 --> 00:43:48.850
and a successful competition,
but we see conductivity

00:43:48.850 --> 00:43:51.070
all over the place,
including in,

00:43:51.070 --> 00:43:55.000
say, moocs, which have
forums and tutorials along

00:43:55.000 --> 00:43:57.640
with guided lessons.

00:43:57.640 --> 00:44:02.710
We think moocs are not adaptive
or computationally intensive,

00:44:02.710 --> 00:44:04.840
so they're not really
taking advantage

00:44:04.840 --> 00:44:08.560
of what technology has to
offer for learning today.

00:44:08.560 --> 00:44:13.760
Our goal with Halite is really
to exploit that possibility.

00:44:13.760 --> 00:44:16.630
So games and global
programming challenges

00:44:16.630 --> 00:44:19.510
running in the
cloud are immersive,

00:44:19.510 --> 00:44:22.030
they're open ended
with potential

00:44:22.030 --> 00:44:25.000
for any number of creative
strategies, as we've heard,

00:44:25.000 --> 00:44:25.960
and they're adaptive.

00:44:25.960 --> 00:44:28.510
So they're suited to
beginners who may just

00:44:28.510 --> 00:44:31.270
be looking to gain confidence
in their first programming

00:44:31.270 --> 00:44:34.120
language, but also provide
the opportunity for more

00:44:34.120 --> 00:44:37.300
experienced players to gain
mastery of a particular skill

00:44:37.300 --> 00:44:38.320
set.

00:44:38.320 --> 00:44:43.420
Really, this is about
engaging with skills and tools

00:44:43.420 --> 00:44:46.040
on your own terms.

00:44:46.040 --> 00:44:50.590
So if we revisit this question
of how humans and machines can

00:44:50.590 --> 00:44:52.540
work together--

00:44:52.540 --> 00:44:54.610
let's go back to Ben
and Michael saying

00:44:54.610 --> 00:44:57.400
that Halite is really about
humans working with machines,

00:44:57.400 --> 00:44:59.920
not competing
against the machine.

00:44:59.920 --> 00:45:02.980
We've seen at this conference
the increasing number of ways

00:45:02.980 --> 00:45:06.060
in which humans and machines
are being paired together

00:45:06.060 --> 00:45:08.560
or have to pair together
to solve problems

00:45:08.560 --> 00:45:10.210
in the real world.

00:45:10.210 --> 00:45:13.690
And the real world, as we
know, is highly uncertain

00:45:13.690 --> 00:45:16.750
and requires flexible
and adaptive solutions.

00:45:16.750 --> 00:45:19.330
So our question
is, to what extent

00:45:19.330 --> 00:45:22.930
can games and collaborative
programming challenges

00:45:22.930 --> 00:45:27.520
like Halite actually
serve as a training ground

00:45:27.520 --> 00:45:33.160
for training and building
intelligent machines?

00:45:33.160 --> 00:45:37.630
So playing a game is
not just about winning,

00:45:37.630 --> 00:45:41.050
it's also about thinking,
through which we learn.

00:45:41.050 --> 00:45:45.550
And with these
games we see there's

00:45:45.550 --> 00:45:47.650
no path one path to learning.

00:45:47.650 --> 00:45:52.360
And at the same time, we may
have goals and specific skills

00:45:52.360 --> 00:45:55.210
that we'd want to
acquire, but those skills

00:45:55.210 --> 00:45:58.180
have many applications.

00:45:58.180 --> 00:46:02.160
So at Two Sigma, we take
learning and education

00:46:02.160 --> 00:46:04.420
very seriously.

00:46:04.420 --> 00:46:07.120
We take problem
solving very seriously.

00:46:07.120 --> 00:46:10.980
And what we ask you
to think about is,

00:46:10.980 --> 00:46:14.920
what are the ways to really
be engaged in your learning?

00:46:14.920 --> 00:46:18.240
We think Halite, the
global AI competition,

00:46:18.240 --> 00:46:21.030
serves a unique
educational function

00:46:21.030 --> 00:46:22.860
with a lot of opportunity.

00:46:22.860 --> 00:46:24.480
We'd love to hear your feedback.

00:46:24.480 --> 00:46:25.980
As Ben and Michael
mentioned, we're

00:46:25.980 --> 00:46:28.710
launching Halite III this
fall and you can sign up.

00:46:28.710 --> 00:46:30.690
But in advance of
the competition,

00:46:30.690 --> 00:46:33.570
we'd also like to design
it to make sure that you

00:46:33.570 --> 00:46:35.400
can achieve your goals.

00:46:35.400 --> 00:46:36.990
So thank you very much.

00:46:36.990 --> 00:46:39.740
[MUSIC PLAYING]

